====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[symbol]] in an [[alphabet]].
#: {{ux|en|There are twenty-six '''letters''' in the English alphabet.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|23|38}}
#*: And a superscription also was written over him in '''letters''' of Greek, and Latin, and Hebrew.
# A written or printed communication, generally longer and more formal than a [[note]].
#: {{ux|en|I wrote a '''letter''' to my sister about my life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|William Walsh (poet)|William Walsh}}|passage=The style of '''letters''' ought to be free, easy, and natural.|title=Letters and Poems, Amorous and Gallant|chapter=Preface|year=1692}}
#* {{quote-book|en|url=https://books.google.com.tr/books?id=LUHKPKjW-ZEC&pg=PA94&dq=%22+you+and+the+young%22+%22subject:%22poetry%22|title=Poet Lore|publisher=AMS Reprint|oclc=6524500|year=1892|volume=4|page=94|passage=The magician gave this to the young man and said to him ,“ Go at such an hour of the night and stand before a pagan tomb and call the demons , and throw the '''letter''' into the air , and immediately they will come to you . ” And the young  man called the devils and threw the '''letter''' into the air, and the prince of darkness came,{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author={{w|Ben Travers}}|title={{w|A Cuckoo in the Nest}}
|chapter=1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1521052W
|passage=He read the '''letter''' aloud. Sophia listened with the studied air of one for whom, even in these days, a title possessed some surreptitious allurement.}}
#* {{RQ:Christie Autobiography|I|4|passage=An indulgent playmate, Grannie would lay aside the long scratchy-looking '''letter''' she was writing (heavily crossed ‘to save notepaper’) and enter into the delightful pastime of ‘a chicken from Mr Whiteley's’.}}
# The [[literal]] [[meaning]] of something, as distinguished from its [[intended]] and [[remote]]r meaning (the {{m|en|spirit}}).
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Jeremy Taylor}}| passage=We must observe the '''letter''' of the law, without doing violence to the reason of the law and the intention of the lawgiver.| year=1650| title=The Rule and Exercises of Holy Living}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|passage=I broke the '''letter''' of it to keep the sense.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 February 2009|work=BBC|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7904886.stm|title=Euro MP expenses 'can reach £1m'|passage=Some MEPs from some countries may have pocketed £2m more than I have by observing the '''letter''' but not the spirit of the rules.}}
# {{qualifier|plural}} [[literature|Literature]].
#: {{ux|en|Benjamin Franklin was multiskilled – a scientist, politician and a man of '''letters'''.}}
# {{lb|en|law}} A [[division]] [[unit]] of a piece of law marked by a letter of the [[alphabet]].
#: {{uxi|en|'''Letter''' (b) constitutes an exception to this provision.}}
# {{lb|en|US|uncountable}} A size of [[paper]], 8½ [[inch|in]] × 11 in (215.9 [[millimetre|mm]] × 279.4 mm, US paper sizes rounded to the nearest 5 mm).
# {{lb|en|Canada|uncountable}} A size of [[paper]], 215 mm × 280 mm.
# {{lb|en|US|scholastic}} {{clipping of|en|varsity letter}}
# {{lb|en|printing|dated}} A single type; type, collectively; a style of type.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=8 February 1644|title=Diary|author={{w|John Evelyn}}|passage=Under these buildings{{...}}was the king's printing house, and that famous '''letter''' so much esteemed.}}

